no daily record lows","records":"Charleston has nearly stopped setting record lows","change":"Charleston's nights have warmed since 1986. Its days cooled over the long run but have warmed since 1986.","rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 46\u00b0F was the 23rd coolest November 13 high (tied) of 123 since 1903, the coolest since 2022.","t_yest":"November 13: cooler than 81% of years","p_yest":"The high of <strong>46\u00b0F</strong> was the 23rd coolest November 13 high (tied) of 123 since 1903, the coolest since 2022. The normal high for the date is 57\u00b0F, and the record is 86\u00b0F (1931). The low of <strong>39\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Charleston has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Charleston's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Charleston has set <strong class=\"sig\">26 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 80 that chance alone would give, and <strong>43 record highs</strong>, fewer than the 80 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (116). Charleston's all-time high is 108\u00b0F, on August 6, 1918.","t_chg":"Charleston's nights have warmed since 1986. Its days cooled over the long run but have warmed since 1986.","p_chg":"Across 115 complete years: Charleston's average low shows no clear change: +0.07\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.01 to +0.14) that includes zero. It's close, but the range still includes zero. Charleston's average high has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.29\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.40 to \u22120.18), about \u22123.5\u00b0F since 1905. July and August highs have edged down across the full record; the 1930s summers were the hottest in it. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.38\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.08 to +0.68) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.52\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.11 to +0.93). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 1 of 12 months. Highs rose in none; 9 months fell.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Charleston's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.22 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 46.2 inches a year. The wettest year was 2018 (67.05 in); the driest, 1930 (26.13 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 6 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}